me idea of  material and energy flows but the system  is a little bit different the system is  typically a cluster of companies an  industrial park something where a group  of companies are all working in the same  vicinity more or less but they have  different inputs and different outputs  from their industrial processes and we  believe that by looking at that  carefully and understanding who has what  inputs and outputs we can create a lot  of efficiencies because we're spining  all over the world that even without  professors this sharing is being done  and with a little bit more guidance we  can increase the rate at which these  companies are willing to share and  therefore conserve resources and  sometimes even gain a new revenue stream  so if you're if you used to sell  something if something used to be  construed as a waste and now it's  construed as a resource then you can  turn that into a revenue stream okay and  I am curious to know what led you to  study this well I'll tell you the truth  a lot of my background before I became a  professor was in waste management and  that's in part because I grew up in the  area in the air I grew up in the arena  where waste and recycling we're starting  to be taken seriously  after Earth Day and so forth and I got  involved in that field accidentally the  way a lot of students do when they take  their first job I work for a recycling  company and I realized just how much we  were throwing away and how little we  were recovering and how much better the  world could be if we if we conserve our  resources so that was really the start  of that and what I realized about waste  is that you sim  they can't solve the waste problem by  only talking about waste waste at the  end of the pipe is at the end of the  system and in order to solve a problem  like waste you have to look at the whole  system back to the raw materials back to  the mines back to the way we process  things the way that we distribute  products and where they land all of this  is a big chain that has to be considered  if we want to reduce waste or not even  create as much waste in the first place  because we're able to put it back into  the industry now you have received  recently a very prestigious award from  remind me of what the agency is the  International Society for industrial  ecology right for work that you have  done with really moving forward the sub  heading of symbiosis so what are some of  the things that you have really worked  on to move that feel forward well of  course from our perspective were we're  interested in improving the principles  that surround these fields so that  people all over the world can can it can  and get engaged with them so let me set  out a couple of examples yet and then I  think it'll be clear we had the most  famous industrial symbiosis example is  in Denmark and what happened without  urging from the government or much other  external behavior the companies in this  small city of kahlenberge  figured out on their own that they could  exchange resources it was the same time  period where people were getting there  some dolt of environmental awareness and  a group of managers who knew each other  from the town who belong to the Rotary  Club whose children played soccer  together recognized that one company's  waste product could easily become  another company's food and so for  example there's a power plant that  has a lot of steam and so the steam  could be used by the pharmaceutical  plant and the refinery and waste from  those projects those industries could be  used in wholly different ways and so  pretty soon we had an image of rather  than waste each of these outputs was  being used in a creative way  sometimes with treatment so for example  the the air pollution stream from the  power plant could actually be captured  as a chemical that is the chemical  behind gypsum so calcium sulfate  so with this gypsum sure enough a gypsum  board company moved in and that created  economic development it created jobs and  it was more efficient for the company  because they could get their raw  materials from right next door so when  we really understood this model and even  the fact that it was self-organized from  people talking with one another that  gave us so much inspiration to say well  this self-organization is an important  principle and we should look for other  basic industrial clusters where such  things would be likely to happen mm-hmm  and and so that took us many years to  kind of sort out and make lists of how  efficient are these plants how are they  looking not only today but on a whole  lifecycle basis and that's one of the  important ideas of industrial ecology is  to look across the whole lifecycle and  how can you minimize the waste streams  and so indeed when we became better  researchers we realized that there are  many ways that this happens in  industrialized countries like China  where so much has made its you know the  factory for the world there we saw that  often times there was a lot of  government direction but it was the same  phenomenon that with a proper committee  and so forth then people could figure  out that there were good solutions for  companies and much larger industrial  parks than the ones we typically see in  other parts of the world where they too  could  and conserve water wastewater solid  waste commercial waste energy flows  steam all of these things were up for  grabs if we could do it right and allow  them to take advantage of the symbiotic  relationship of sharing rather than just  kind of ignoring each other in the in  the in this general industrial park  mm-hmm we for many years we visited  Puerto Rico because it's nearby  it has a strong industrial heritage in  these last 30 or 40 years and there we  could see everything sometimes there  would be a couple of guys driving their  Mustangs into the plant in the morning  and well they just got to talking and  pretty soon there was some kind of 