d what  kind of waste it has and we have some  great databases we're the only ones that  have them were able to create the GIS  that will show us but even then there  are always obstacles so that's an  ambitious example I've been very  impressed with what happens in emerging  market countries where waste is a  material waste is if you don't have a  lot if you if you're in a poor country  this need to throw out things is not as  is relevant at akai and so we looked at  a site in India this is our best result  ever in our research where there were 45  companies in an industrial park not that  far from Bangalore India you know that's  the Silicon Valley of India and just  because again there was some social  capital people knew each other they  worked on different things together they  had lunches and so forth that these  companies were doing a lot of symbiotic  things and because we were willing to  study it in detail  we actually got from all 45 countries  everything that's coming into their  plant everything that stays in their  plant everything that goes out of their  plant and when we added that all up  there was a million tons of what may  people would call waste and what we  would call you know material out of  place so when we actually finished the  analysis and saw that so much of it  actually was being recaptured by another  facility or sometimes within the same  facility that 99.5% of that million tons  was being reused or recycled at least  once Wow  I mean that is a circular economy it  certainly is and because industrial  ecology students at Yale are quite  adventurous some of them took it upon  themselves to find out where that last  little did they find out where was it  going well it was so interesting you  know we look we interview a factory  owner and he'd say well you know here's  how we do this and here's where our  materials go and that pile mr. Kumar  comes in at five o'clock and picks it up  well where does that pile go and so  first we have a saying in industrial  ecology that industrial colleges follow  the materials yes so my students were  jumping in taxis and cabs and following  these people through town and actually  yes finding out just what was happening  that even the way even the part that was  not usable by the 45 companies in the  industrial park was usable to somebody  right and the sale of that was what put  breakfast on the table the next morning  for their kids even if it was just a few  rupees say that was a great lesson to us  that waste has more value in a  developing country typically but also  that if you treat it right it at least  you can use the value that is there that  it is worth trying to make it happen  yeah so I know you're doing work in  Africa as well tell us what you're doing  there  well I've worked in several countries  but the one that we're working on right  now is in Rwanda okay and Rwanda there  were working on apparel clothing it's a  really interesting issue and I'll try to  explain it briefly but I tend to get  carried away when I talk about it  you you know from watching the TV and  reading the news that so many of our  Western clothing you know our clothing  that we don't want anymore  get shipped to African countries and in  other countries but East Africa is a  real place where they go is so many  articles and garments of clothing so  what we're looking at is some of the  East African countries are saying we  don't want this anymore we used to have  a modest amount and it was nice but now  there's so much which we do and Rwanda  is really leading that charge in East  Africa and they're saying why should we  take this clothing it would it denies  our ability to have a domestic market  and we want to employ all our people and  move up the economic scale so let's have  domestic industries and so they've been  working on domestic industries and  they've put a huge tariffs and the youth  clothing that's coming in from Germany  and UK and us and so forth so that's  discouraging a youth clothing from  coming in on the other hand a China is  very active in Africa as we know and  they have a factory there they also  bring in a lot of less expensive new  clothing and of course Rwanda wants to  make its own clothing and even has a law  called made in Rwanda because they want  to make everything in Rwanda so that  people can have jobs so you have this  interesting superpower clash at the  level of China and the u.s. because  under the trump administration Tareq we  had a little tariff battle about East  African clothing even so that's been a  prominent yo tool for for the youth for  for incentives there so we look at kind  of the you know the superpower level and  we look at the East Africa regional  level you know it turns out that  Tanzania and Kenya are handling this  differently than Rwanda and we want to  see if the very bold experiments in  Rwanda can work out and market that  clothing can be made at a market rate  that's affordable to the just some very  poor people there and so all of this it  relates to Industrial ecology because  we're thing  so much about reuse of clothing reused  clothing comes in from from the West and  of course the West thinks that's a great  economic and environmental solution  right but that's not how it's perceived  and so how well what else can we do  to close the loop right and and that's  really the part that brings an  industrial ecology of course we work  with development economists and others  who also want to keep track of the  economics but what a fascinating  application for us for industrial  ecology because Rwanda it says right out  loud we're trying to create a circular  economy where we don't have the waste  and we are reusing it we don't have a  linear economy but we have a circular  economy where clothing and many other  goods cycle right and that's the  exciting work that we're working on this  semester and what about next steps  well I think that what we see in the  future is an interesting blend of  technology high technology the need for  jobs going hate going wanting I just  came from a presentation about high-tech  knitting machines for apparel and these  are typically happening in Asia and  Europe they're very high speed you know  you can program them so they're very  accurate and making the the knitted  goods and and the speaker started out by  saying well you know when we cut a piece  of a garment about 30 percent of it goes  to waste and so I thought well there's a  big waste stream here but let's keep  talking let's keep looking at these  knitting machines and of course by the  end the Knitting machines are are  endlessly for you ask me about the  future you know they're there they're  there capabilities will go up their  prices will come down a little bit but  at the end of life and this is still  true because industrial occultists are  always thinking about systems systems  science you know always considering at  the end of life  there's 85% disposal to landfills  mm-hmm so you get these great knitting  machines  there's a bunch of ways you go through  the whole system of the supply chains  and so forth and then you still get to a  place where there's very little to do  and no one's you know jumping on it  enough to figure out what do we do at  the end of life when we're landfilling  so much yeah all over the world exactly  we need to start at the beginning and  say less is more less is more or let's  design a system not just an entry point  where these these types of manufacturers  can make money but you know and leave  out what happens to the earth and the  last part so I would say that these  kinds of experiences fortify me as a  professor and as former president of our  industrial ecology society to say you  know these are some open fronts we can't  look at these only and with this lens  but we need multiple lenses you can see  that although I've stressed the physical  side of this here in Rwanda it becomes a  social problem you