eresting blend of  technology high technology the need for  jobs going hate going wanting I just  came from a presentation about high-tech  knitting machines for apparel and these  are typically happening in Asia and  Europe they're very high speed you know  you can program them so they're very  accurate and making the the knitted  goods and and the speaker started out by  saying well you know when we cut a piece  of a garment about 30 percent of it goes  to waste and so I thought well there's a  big waste stream here but let's keep  talking let's keep looking at these  knitting machines and of course by the  end the Knitting machines are are  endlessly for you ask me about the  future you know they're there they're  there capabilities will go up their  prices will come down a little bit but  at the end of life and this is still  true because industrial occultists are  always thinking about systems systems  science you know always considering at  the end of life  there's 85% disposal to landfills  mm-hmm so you get these great knitting  machines  there's a bunch of ways you go through  the whole system of the supply chains  and so forth and then you still get to a  place where there's very little to do  and no one's you know jumping on it  enough to figure out what do we do at  the end of life when we're landfilling  so much yeah all over the world exactly  we need to start at the beginning and  say less is more less is more or let's  design a system not just an entry point  where these these types of manufacturers  can make money but you know and leave  out what happens to the earth and the  last part so I would say that these  kinds of experiences fortify me as a  professor and as former president of our  industrial ecology society to say you  know these are some open fronts we can't  look at these only and with this lens  but we need multiple lenses you can see  that although I've stressed the physical  side of this here in Rwanda it becomes a  social problem you know where do poor  people get their clothing and how will  it cycle to them right and how do we  consider these things that spring right  out of the question of resource  management right because resources might  get managed physically but then what  happens and that's where we interface so  much with the social sciences which  makes industrial ecology such a rich and  useful enterprise right right it  certainly is I mean you know I think  we're at a crisis point with so many so  much trash in the world and the oceans  plastic it's quite a problem so yeah and  it's getting a lot of attention right  now so you know you're interviewing me  I'm grateful but when people didn't  really care about waste and plastics and  so forth you know you kind of just have  a silent period for a few years and and  now it's just so fascinating to be the  phone's ringing the media's calling and  it's really the heart of that is not  having a systems approach to our to our  materials right exactly  well thank you so much for being here  and sharing some of your work with us  it's my pleasure I love to share this  work and you were wonderful too  thank you for more information about  professor certo and her research please  visit our website at macmillan report  yale.edu be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

